1. FFmpeg  (libavformat, libavcodec, libswscale, libswresample, libavutil)
================================================================================

BENCsnip reads and writes media through the FFmpeg libraries. It does not
include their source; a build links whatever prefix it was pointed at, either
the system packages or a static one in vendor/ffmpeg.

FFmpeg is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or
later (LGPL v2.1+). Some optional components — notably libx264 and libx265 —
are GPL, and configuring FFmpeg with --enable-gpl makes the resulting libraries
GPL v2+ as a whole.

    FFmpeg <https://ffmpeg.org/>
    Copyright (c) 2000-2026 the FFmpeg developers
    Licensed under the LGPL v2.1 or later; see
    <https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html> and the COPYING files in the FFmpeg source.

**What this means for a binary you hand to someone.** The LGPL requires that
the recipient be able to replace the FFmpeg part with their own build. Two ways
to satisfy that, and BENCsnip is arranged so either works:

  * **Link the system FFmpeg** (the default when no vendor/ffmpeg exists). The
    libraries stay shared objects the user already owns and can replace. Nothing
    further is required beyond this notice.

  * **Ship a static build.** Then the relink requirement applies to you: publish
    the FFmpeg source and configure line you used, alongside the BENCsnip object
    files or a build that reproduces the link. `tools/build-ffmpeg.sh` records
    the exact configure line it used in vendor/ffmpeg/CONFIGURE, for this
    reason. If that build enabled GPL components, the whole distributed binary
    must be offered under the GPL — BENCsnip's own MIT terms permit this, but
    your obligations then follow the GPL, not the MIT license above.

The default `tools/build-ffmpeg.sh` configuration is LGPL-only, which is the
choice that keeps this simple. Pass `--gpl` to it and the note above applies.

